Closures: (About Closures in general but the focus is on Java and C++)
=========
These are the concepts that are involved.

[a] Capture Site:
This is the site in the code where the closure is constructed. It could capture variables
from the surrounding scope or not. After capture it is assigned to a Call Signature
variable.

[b] Call Site:
This is where the Lambda is actually executed. It does not have to be in the same scope
as [a] above.

[c] Call Signature
This is the signature of the variables that expect lambdas. For C++ it is a Function object
or function pointer (if no capture is involved). For Java it is an interface with a single
method (thank goodness no new syntax)

[d] Capture Signature (internal)
This is applied at the Capture Site [a] and is used to describe what variables from the surrounding
scope are being captured. This could be implicit (Java) or explicit (C++)

For Java, this signature is internal to the compiler and contains the method handle, its static input
types, its static return types, its dynamic input types. Dynamic input types are essentially type
inferences of the capture variables. The setup of this signature and the capture logic is done just one
and all future capture site usages are free.

